Build a Community ID (version 1) calculator that matches Suricata's EVE community_id field for IPv4 and IPv6 flows in `/app/src/output-json.c`. Downstream IR tooling needs the same bidirectional flow key this engine would log so Zeek and EVE records can join. Do not modify files under `/app`.

Write an executable command-line tool at `/results/community-id` (Python 3 with a shebang, or a gcc-built binary). It reads UTF-8 JSON lines from stdin. Each input line is one object with keys `seed` (integer 0-65535), `src_ip` (IPv4 or IPv6 text), `dst_ip`, `proto` (integer IP protocol number), `sp` (integer), and `dp` (integer). For ICMP (proto 1) and ICMPv6 (proto 58), `sp` and `dp` are the ICMP type values stored on the flow, not L4 ports. Apply the same packed layout, endianness, padding, endpoint ordering, hashing, and encoding this engine uses when community-id is enabled.

Print one UTF-8 JSON object per input line to stdout with keys in this order: `seed`, `src_ip`, `dst_ip`, `proto`, `sp`, `dp`, `community_id` (string). No extra keys. Compact JSON with no space after `:` or `,`. End each line with a single newline. Each stdin line for the tool is one well-formed object with those six keys; echo `src_ip` and `dst_ip` exactly as given.

Also write `/results/community_id.jsonl` containing the tool's stdout for exactly these 12 input lines, in this order (one JSON object per line, same compact schema as the tool's output):

